Let the first and second cyclists' speeds be u and v, and let t be the time to the meeting. The first cyclist's remaining distance is the distance the second cyclist covered before meeting, so 40u=vt. The second cyclist's remaining distance is ut, giving 90v=ut.
Multiplying gives (40u)(90v)=(vt)(ut), hence t²=3600 and t=60 minutes. From 40u=60v, u/v=3/2.
FINAL_ANSWER: \boxed{60\text{ minutes};\ 3:2}

For two plays, call the best play A and the other B. There are 2n mothers, exactly n honest, and n who vote for their own child's play. With an honest mother always voting A, A loses its strict majority only if all n dishonest mothers are among the n mothers whose children are in B; then the result is a tie. If the honest set is chosen uniformly among all size-n subsets, this exceptional event has probability 1/binom(2n,n). Therefore (a) the probability is 1 − 1/binom(2n,n).
For more than two plays, the problem does not specify the number of plays, how the best play is determined among them, nor a precise probability model for which mothers are honest. Even assuming a uniformly random honest subset, the probability depends on the number of plays (and on how ties are treated), so there is no single numerical answer to (b) from the supplied statement.
FINAL_ANSWER: \boxed{(a) 1-1/\binom{2n}{n}; (b) not determined as stated}

