Let P=(x,y) lie on y=-b-x^2. The second equation is equivalent to (x-2a)^2+(y-2a)^2=4, so P must be at distance 2 from some point (2a,2a) on y=x. As this center moves along the line, the possible distances range from the perpendicular distance to the line upward without bound. Thus an a exists exactly when |x-y|/sqrt(2)≤2.
Substituting y gives |x^2+x+b|≤2sqrt(2) for some real x. The quadratic has range [b-1/4,∞), which meets [-2sqrt(2),2sqrt(2)] exactly when b-1/4≤2sqrt(2); there is no lower restriction.
FINAL_ANSWER: \boxed{b\leq \frac14+2\sqrt2}

