For part (b), multiplying any n by a prime not dividing it strictly decreases φ(n)/n, hence produces a greater efficiency. Thus a maximum does not exist.
FINAL_ANSWER: \boxed{a)\ 30030\text{ works; smallest not established};\quad b)\ no}

Write the roots as 5^a and 5^b with integers 0<=a<b. The monic quadratic is uniquely x^2-(5^a+5^b)x+5^(a+b). The coefficient bound B=122^20 satisfies 5^59 < B < 5^60, so the product condition is exactly a+b<=59; it also ensures the sum coefficient is at most B, since 5^a+5^b<=2*5^b and, for b<=59, this is below B. (For b=59, the sharper bound 5^59+5^58 < B holds.)
For each b from 1 through 59, a ranges from 0 through min(b-1,59-b). Summing these counts gives 869. Distinct exponent pairs give distinct root pairs and hence distinct quadratics.
FINAL_ANSWER: \boxed{869}

