At a vertex, the cheese within distance \(1\) is the part of the unit ball cut out by the polyhedron's local corner. If that corner has solid angle \(\Omega\), this piece has volume \(\Omega/3\): in spherical coordinates its volume is \(\int_0^1 r^2\,dr\) times the solid angle.

For any convex polyhedron, the solid angles of all its vertices sum to \(4\pi\), as the vertex cones partition directions on the unit sphere (up to boundaries). Thus the sum of the eaten volumes is \((1/3)4\pi=4\pi/3\) for each farmer. The stated nonoverlap ensures these pieces add without double-counting. The shapes and vertex counts do not change the total.

FINAL_ANSWER: \boxed{1\text{ times (equal damage)}}

Every city needs an outgoing white road, since a journey to another city must start with white. Also, every city needs an outgoing black road: if a city had no black departure, then every alternating journey that reaches it via a white road would have to stop there, preventing it from serving as an intermediate city. The requirement that all ordered pairs be connectable forces both colors to depart from each city, so there are at least 2·100 roads.

This bound is attained. Number cities modulo 100. For every i, make a white road i→i+1 and a black road i→i+2. These are distinct roads. Starting at i, travel white then black repeatedly; each pair of roads advances by 3. Because 3 is relatively prime to 100, the cities reached after an even number of roads include every city. Thus every destination is reachable, using an alternating journey that begins white.
FINAL_ANSWER: \boxed{200}

