Let G, L, and R denote gymnasium, lyceum, and regular-school students. If a G student speaks, “I am a G” is true, so the right neighbor cannot be R, to whom G students always lie. If the speaker is L, the statement is false, and it can be uttered as a lie only when the right neighbor is G; hence L must be followed by G. Similarly, a speaker R must be followed by L.
If any R occurs in the circle, following rightward we get R, then L, then G. But no G can be followed by R. Consequently there is no way for this chain of forced transitions to return to an R; since the arrangement is a circle, R cannot occur at all.
