For positive integers, the equation is equivalent to log(x)/x=log(y)/y. This function rises to e and then strictly falls. The only distinct positive integers with the same value are 2 and 4; indeed 2^4=4^2.
For rationals put r=y/x. Taking logarithms gives x^(r-1)=r. Hence x=r^(1/(r-1)) and y=r^(r/(r-1)); take all positive rational r≠1 for which both values are rational. Conversely these formulas give y/x=r and x^r=y, proving the equation. Both orders are included.
FINAL_ANSWER: \boxed{(2,4),(4,2);quad (x,y)=(r^{1/(r-1)},r^{r/(r-1)})\text{ with }r\in\mathbb Q_{>0},r\ne1\text{ and }x,y\in\mathbb Q.}

